B: 4021 ns per operation (median)
A needs 1.1% more time than B: B takes only 0.99 times as long as A.
With 95% confidence the difference lies between 2.86% more time and 2.42% less time (B takes between 0.972 and 1.02 times as long as A).
Noise floor: 0.791%. Identical code measured against itself on this setup can look that different, so smaller differences mean nothing.
Neighbouring measurements are nearly independent (autocorrelation -0.39), so they were resampled one by one.

Verdict: NOT RESOLVED. This run did not establish which candidate is faster. That is not the same as "equally fast": the warnings below say what is missing.

Warnings:
  - the interval [-2.86%, 2.42%] includes zero, so a difference in either direction is consistent with these measurements
  - the program holds 18 MB of live data, more than the caches of many machines; for data that size, where it lies in memory can shift the result by several points in ways the interval does not cover, and differently in the next process; run the comparison in several processes with the multiproc package

